## Diagram 1: Keyless Trust, No Stored Key

Placement: After the "## Why Keyless" heading, before its bullet list
Alt text: Planton signs a short-lived token for one connection; the customer's cloud account checks it against a trust the customer created and returns short-lived credentials, with no key stored in Planton
Style: conceptual

### Prompt:
Create a left-to-right conceptual diagram with two large regions side by side.

Key elements:
- Left region labeled "Planton" containing one element labeled "Identity issuer: https://planton.ai" and, beneath it, a small element labeled "Published keys"
- A token card moving from the left region to the right region, labeled "Token for one connection (15 minutes)"
- Right region labeled "Your cloud account" containing an element labeled "Your trust: issuer + audience + subject" and, beside it, an element labeled "Role / service account / app registration"
- An arrow from "Your trust" back to "Published keys" labeled "checks the signature"
- An arrow from "Your trust" to "Role / service account / app registration" labeled "short-lived credentials"
- Inside the left region, an empty dashed box labeled "No stored key" with a crossed-out key icon

Visual style:
- Dark background (#1f2937) with light text
- Blue accent color (#60a5fa) for the Planton region and the token card
- Amber (#f59e0b) for the trust element in the customer's account
- Gray (#9ca3af) for secondary arrows
- Clean, minimal, professional
- No decorative elements, no cloud vendor logos

Context: This diagram explains, to a platform or security engineer evaluating Planton, that a keyless connection keeps no key in Planton: the customer's own cloud decides, per connection, whether to accept Planton's short-lived token.
